Good question—this is exactly what you need for an Excel-like header 👍

You want:

Code | Supplier | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 ...


---

✅ Correct Way to Combine Static + Dynamic Columns

Instead of using nested Table(), build a single flat collection that includes:

Static columns (Code, Supplier)

Dynamic columns (Questions)



---

🔧 Step 1: Build Header Collection (OnVisible)

Replace your current logic with:

Clear(colHeader);

// Static columns
Collect(colHeader, {Value: "Code"});
Collect(colHeader, {Value: "Supplier"});

// Dynamic columns (Questions)
Collect(
    colHeader,
    AddColumns(
        Distinct('SE Question List', Title),
        "Value",
        Result
    )
);


---

🔧 Step 2: Bind to Gallery

Gallery_Header.Items = colHeader


---

🔧 Step 3: Label Text

lbl_QuestionTitle.Text = ThisItem.Value


---

🔧 Step 4: Width Control (Optional but Recommended)

If you want different widths:

lbl_QuestionTitle.Width =
Switch(
    ThisItem.Value,
    "Code", 120,
    "Supplier", 200,
    200   // Questions
)


---

🔧 Step 5: Gallery Width (IMPORTANT for Scroll)

Gallery_Header.LayoutMinWidth =
Sum(
    colHeader,
    Switch(
        Value,
        "Code", 120,
        "Supplier", 200,
        200
    )
)


---

⚠️ Important Notes

❌ Don’t do this:

Table(
    {Value:"Code"},
    {Value:"Supplier"},
    Distinct(...)
)

👉 This causes invalid structure / nested tables


---

✅ Always flatten using:

Collect()

AddColumns()



---

💡 Pro Tip (Best Practice)

Add a Type column so you can control behavior later:

Clear(colHeader);

// Static
Collect(colHeader, {Value: "Code", Type: "Static"});
Collect(colHeader, {Value: "Supplier", Type: "Static"});

// Dynamic
Collect(
    colHeader,
    AddColumns(
        Distinct('SE Question List', Title),
        "Value", Result,
        "Type", "Question"
    )
);

Then you can do:

If(ThisItem.Type = "Static", Bold, Normal)


